/* ==================== Script 1239 id="amiyo-export8-step04-verified" ==================== */
/* Export plan 2026-11b, step 4 of 20 (audio), checked with real exports (headless Chrome) and the files read back with ffmpeg / ffprobe.
   Checked and right: the audio-only WAV mix (clip fades, volume, track fader, hard-left pan, two tracks at once) matched a model of the sums to 0.1 dB; "Make the loudest point -1 dB" gave exactly -1.00 dBFS; the loudness targets -14, -16 and -23 LUFS were read back with ffmpeg ebur128 as -14.0, -16.0 and -23.0 (stereo 48 kHz 16 and 24 bit, mono 44.1 kHz); 16 kHz mono; sample rate, bit depth, channels and length right in every file. In the video export (WebM VP9 + Opus) the sound of a video clip with its own volume, two sound tracks with different faders and pans, a keyframed volume line, fades and a muted track (left out) matched the audio-only mix to 0.002 of full scale (about 0.1 dB) at 17 points.
   Faults found and fixed: (1) the audio-only export could only save WAV. It now has a File type choice: WAV (full quality) or MP3 (small file, 128-320 kbit/s; 64-160 below 32 kHz, where MP3 allows no more). The MP3 encoder is built into this file, so it works offline. MP3 keeps all of the above (fades, pan, loudness targets): read back as MP3 48 kHz 192 kbit/s, 44.1 kHz mono 128, 16 kHz mono (320 asked, 160 used), 22.05 kHz (192 asked, 160 used), -16 LUFS target read back as -15.9. Your choice is remembered. (2) A sound track with a pan other than centre forced the video export out of the fast export into the real-time recorder, which on a busy phone can drop sound and skip fades (a test export had no fade-in, no fade-out and was 0.33 s too long). The fast export now applies the track pan itself, exactly like the audio export: pan -0.8 and +1 matched to 0.001. Ducking still uses the recorder. (3) The audio file name turned every letter outside A-Z into "_" (a Hindi project name became "_ _ test_1-audio.wav"); names in any language now keep their letters and the extension is .wav or .mp3.
   Not tested here: MP4/H.264 audio (this test browser has none, the fast exporter's AAC path is the same mixer), ducking and clip audio effects in the real-time recorder, a 10 minute+ MP3 (pieces of 4 minutes are encoded one after another, the WAV path of the same loop was checked in an earlier round). */window.AMIYO_EXPORT8_STEP=4;try{window.AMIYO_EDITOR_BUILD="export8-step04-audio";}catch(e){}

